#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "Each batch publishes what it changed next to its transaction, in data blobs: the new vote ids, every ballot it wrote (still encrypted) and the new encrypted total. The batch program lays this data out itself, so what is published is exactly what was proven. With it anyone can rebuild an election’s state, but the network deletes the blobs after about two weeks."
msgstr "Each batch publishes what it changed next to its transaction, in data blobs: the new vote ids, every ballot it wrote (still encrypted) and the new encrypted total. The batch program lays this data out itself, so what is published is exactly what was proven. With it anyone can rebuild an election’s state, but the network deletes the blobs after about two weeks."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "What a data blob is"
msgstr "What a data blob is"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "A <0>data blob</0> is a block of data sent along with a transaction. The chain keeps only a fingerprint of each blob (its <1>versioned hash</1>). The data itself is kept by the network’s beacon nodes for about 15 days on Gnosis Chain and about 18 on Ethereum mainnet, then deleted. Sequencers keep the blobs they saw and serve them too."
msgstr "A <0>data blob</0> is a block of data sent along with a transaction. The chain keeps only a fingerprint of each blob (its <1>versioned hash</1>). The data itself is kept by the network’s beacon nodes for about 15 days on Gnosis Chain and about 18 on Ethereum mainnet, then deleted. Sequencers keep the blobs they saw and serve them too."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "An EIP-4844 blob is 4096 cells of 32 bytes, each a BLS12-381 field element, carried next to a transaction rather than in its calldata. The versioned hash is derived from the blob’s <0>KZG commitment</0>. On Gnosis Chain beacon nodes prune blobs after 16384 epochs of 80 s."
msgstr "An EIP-4844 blob is 4096 cells of 32 bytes, each a BLS12-381 field element, carried next to a transaction rather than in its calldata. The versioned hash is derived from the blob’s <0>KZG commitment</0>. On Gnosis Chain beacon nodes prune blobs after 16384 epochs of 80 s."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "What a batch publishes"
msgstr "What a batch publishes"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "The transaction that records a batch carries its data blobs. They hold, in order:"
msgstr "The transaction that records a batch carries its data blobs. They hold, in order:"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "the number of new vote ids, then the vote ids in ascending order;"
msgstr "the number of new vote ids, then the vote ids in ascending order;"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "the number of ballots written (slot updates), then each one in ascending slot order: its slot, then its encrypted answers, two cells per answer;"
msgstr "the number of ballots written (slot updates), then each one in ascending slot order: its slot, then its encrypted answers, two cells per answer;"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "the new encrypted total, two cells per ballot field;"
msgstr "the new encrypted total, two cells per ballot field;"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "zeros to the end of the last blob."
msgstr "zeros to the end of the last blob."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "New votes, changed votes and silent refreshes are all slot updates in one sorted list, so they look the same. A slot’s first write is still public, because refreshes only touch slots already in use; with a list of voters the slot follows from the address, so who voted and when is public. What stays hidden is which slots in use were changed and which were only refreshed."
msgstr "New votes, changed votes and silent refreshes are all slot updates in one sorted list, so they look the same. A slot’s first write is still public, because refreshes only touch slots already in use; with a list of voters the slot follows from the address, so who voted and when is public. What stays hidden is which slots in use were changed and which were only refreshed."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "A large batch needs several blobs, at most 32, and all of them travel in the one transaction that records it. On Gnosis Chain a block takes at most 2 blobs, so a sequencer sizes each batch to fit the chain’s blob limit and leaves the rest for the next batch."
msgstr "A large batch needs several blobs, at most 32, and all of them travel in the one transaction that records it. On Gnosis Chain a block takes at most 2 blobs, so a sequencer sizes each batch to fit the chain’s blob limit and leaves the rest for the next batch."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "A slot update is the slot key followed by the ballot’s active ciphertexts, each point compressed to one cell. A batch with <0/> cells needs <1/> blobs."
msgstr "A slot update is the slot key followed by the ballot’s active ciphertexts, each point compressed to one cell. A batch with <0/> cells needs <1/> blobs."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "Built by the proof, not trusted"
msgstr "Built by the proof, not trusted"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "The sequencer cannot publish just any data. The batch program lays out the blob contents itself, from the state it has just checked, and publishes a fingerprint of them among its public values. The registry checks that the blobs in the transaction match that fingerprint, so the blobs a transaction carries are exactly the ones the proof covers."
msgstr "The sequencer cannot publish just any data. The batch program lays out the blob contents itself, from the state it has just checked, and publishes a fingerprint of them among its public values. The registry checks that the blobs in the transaction match that fingerprint, so the blobs a transaction carries are exactly the ones the proof covers."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "For each blob the guest evaluates the blob polynomial at a point bound to this process, this state root and this blob’s commitment: the SHA-256 of the process id padded to 32 bytes, the state root before the batch with its bytes reversed and the 48-byte commitment, reduced modulo <0/>, the order of the BLS12-381 scalar field:"
msgstr "For each blob the guest evaluates the blob polynomial at a point bound to this process, this state root and this blob’s commitment: the SHA-256 of the process id padded to 32 bytes, the state root before the batch with its bytes reversed and the 48-byte commitment, reduced modulo <0/>, the order of the BLS12-381 scalar field:"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "It publishes the blob count and the <0>blob digest</0> among its public values:"
msgstr "It publishes the blob count and the <0>blob digest</0> among its public values:"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "The registry recomputes that digest from the commitments and evaluations in the transaction and asks the point-evaluation precompile whether each blob of the transaction opens to <0/> at <1/>."
msgstr "The registry recomputes that digest from the commitments and evaluations in the transaction and asks the point-evaluation precompile whether each blob of the transaction opens to <0/> at <1/>."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "Why it matters"
msgstr "Why it matters"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "Anyone can rebuild an election’s state from its published data alone. That is how sequencers that did not record a batch follow along, and how an observer checks every batch without trusting the sequencer that sent it."
msgstr "Anyone can rebuild an election’s state from its published data alone. That is how sequencers that did not record a batch follow along, and how an observer checks every batch without trusting the sequencer that sent it."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "A voter can find their vote id in the published data of the batch that included it."
msgstr "A voter can find their vote id in the published data of the batch that included it."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "A node started after an election’s blobs were deleted cannot rebuild it from the beacon, so someone has to keep a node or an archive running for the whole election."
msgstr "A node started after an election’s blobs were deleted cannot rebuild it from the beacon, so someone has to keep a node or an archive running for the whole election."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "In this explorer"
msgstr "In this explorer"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "Each batch page downloads the batch’s data blobs from the beacon, or from a configured sequencer once the beacon has deleted them, and decodes them. A blob from the beacon is tied to the transaction by its fingerprint; a blob from a sequencer’s archive is tied by position only, and the page says which."
msgstr "Each batch page downloads the batch’s data blobs from the beacon, or from a configured sequencer once the beacon has deleted them, and decodes them. A blob from the beacon is tied to the transaction by its fingerprint; a blob from a sequencer’s archive is tied by position only, and the page says which."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "A blob from the beacon is tied to the transaction because its KZG commitment hashes to one of the transaction’s versioned hashes. The explorer does not recompute KZG commitments from the bytes."
msgstr "A blob from the beacon is tied to the transaction because its KZG commitment hashes to one of the transaction’s versioned hashes. The explorer does not recompute KZG commitments from the bytes."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "The data blobs of a recent batch, decoded"
msgstr "The data blobs of a recent batch, decoded"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Blobs.tsx
msgid "Next: <0>what the chain checks for each batch</0>."
msgstr "Next: <0>what the chain checks for each batch</0>."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "Every election has a list of voters: who may vote, and with what weight. It can be a fixed list, a list the organizer can update, a list kept by a contract, or a service that signs each voter’s credential. Each voter’s ballot is kept in one place that follows from who they are, so a new vote from them replaces the old one."
msgstr "Every election has a list of voters: who may vote, and with what weight. It can be a fixed list, a list the organizer can update, a list kept by a contract, or a service that signs each voter’s credential. Each voter’s ballot is kept in one place that follows from who they are, so a new vote from them replaces the old one."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "Four kinds of list"
msgstr "Four kinds of list"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "The <0>list of voters</0> says who may vote and with what weight. The organizer picks one of four kinds (the <1>census origin</1>) when creating the election. The proof of each batch checks every voter against the list, and the registry checks that the list used is one the election accepts."
msgstr "The <0>list of voters</0> says who may vote and with what weight. The organizer picks one of four kinds (the <1>census origin</1>) when creating the election. The proof of each batch checks every voter against the list, and the registry checks that the list used is one the election accepts."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "Origin"
msgstr "Origin"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "The list of voters"
msgstr "The list of voters"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "What a batch may be checked against"
msgstr "What a batch may be checked against"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "1 · Fixed list"
msgstr "1 · Fixed list"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "A list of voters, downloaded from the address the election names."
msgstr "A list of voters, downloaded from the address the election names."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "The list fixed at creation."
msgstr "The list fixed at creation."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "2 · Updatable list"
msgstr "2 · Updatable list"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "The same, but the organizer can replace it (<0>setProcessCensus</0>)."
msgstr "The same, but the organizer can replace it (<0>setProcessCensus</0>)."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "The current list only."
msgstr "The current list only."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "3 · List kept by a contract"
msgstr "3 · List kept by a contract"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "A contract keeps the list; sequencers rebuild it from the contract’s <0>CensusMemberAdded</0> logs."
msgstr "A contract keeps the list; sequencers rebuild it from the contract’s <0>CensusMemberAdded</0> logs."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "Any version that was still the contract’s current list at or after the block that created the election."
msgstr "Any version that was still the contract’s current list at or after the block that created the election."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "4 · Credential service provider"
msgstr "4 · Credential service provider"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "A credential service (CSP) signs each voter’s credential; its address stands for the list."
msgstr "A credential service (CSP) signs each voter’s credential; its address stands for the list."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "The provider’s address."
msgstr "The provider’s address."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "For origins 1 to 3 the census is a lean-IMT <0>Merkle tree</0>, and a batch is checked against its root, the <1>census root</1>. For origin 4 the census root is the CSP’s address."
msgstr "For origins 1 to 3 the census is a lean-IMT <0>Merkle tree</0>, and a batch is checked against its root, the <1>census root</1>. For origin 4 the census root is the CSP’s address."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "With a list (origins 1 to 3), each voter shows they are on it with a short proof, and the batch proof checks it for every voter in the batch."
msgstr "With a list (origins 1 to 3), each voter shows they are on it with a short proof, and the batch proof checks it for every voter in the batch."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "<0>Origin 2.</0> Only the organizer can replace the list, keeping the same origin, and only while the election is Ready or Paused and before its end. Batches proven against the previous list are no longer recorded, and a pending vote whose entry in the list changed ends in an error and has to be cast again."
msgstr "<0>Origin 2.</0> Only the organizer can replace the list, keeping the same origin, and only while the election is Ready or Paused and before its end. Batches proven against the previous list are no longer recorded, and a pending vote whose entry in the list changed ends in an error and has to be cast again."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "<0>Origin 3.</0> For every batch the registry asks the contract that keeps the list up to which block the batch’s version was current. That block must be a real one, not in the future and not before the election was created. A version recorded before the election but still current when it was created counts. So the contract must never drop a version, or batches proven against it would no longer be recorded. Weights must stay fixed too, a sequencer rule: a weight change makes the sequencer mark the list unusable."
msgstr "<0>Origin 3.</0> For every batch the registry asks the contract that keeps the list up to which block the batch’s version was current. That block must be a real one, not in the future and not before the election was created. A version recorded before the election but still current when it was created counts. So the contract must never drop a version, or batches proven against it would no longer be recorded. Weights must stay fixed too, a sequencer rule: a weight change makes the sequencer mark the list unusable."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "A voter proves membership with a lean-IMT (Poseidon) inclusion proof. Replacing an origin-2 census is <0>setProcessCensus</0>, which emits <1>CensusUpdated</1>. For origin 3 the registry calls <2>getRootBlockNumber(root)</2> on the census contract on every settlement. It returns the last block the root was valid: the current block for the current root, the block it was replaced in for an older one, and 0 for a root the contract never held. It must be non-zero, not in the future and not before the process’s creation block. The contract has to be append-only, since a batch proven against an evicted root would stop settling."
msgstr "A voter proves membership with a lean-IMT (Poseidon) inclusion proof. Replacing an origin-2 census is <0>setProcessCensus</0>, which emits <1>CensusUpdated</1>. For origin 3 the registry calls <2>getRootBlockNumber(root)</2> on the census contract on every settlement. It returns the last block the root was valid: the current block for the current root, the block it was replaced in for an older one, and 0 for a root the contract never held. It must be non-zero, not in the future and not before the process’s creation block. The contract has to be append-only, since a batch proven against an evicted root would stop settling."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "Credential service providers"
msgstr "Credential service providers"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "With origin 4 there is no list. A <0>credential service provider</0> signs each voter’s credential, the batch proof checks the signature, and the registry checks that the signer is the election’s provider. A credential, once signed, cannot be revoked."
msgstr "With origin 4 there is no list. A <0>credential service provider</0> signs each voter’s credential, the batch proof checks the signature, and the registry checks that the signer is the election’s provider. A credential, once signed, cannot be revoked."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "Each voter presents an ECDSA (secp256k1) signature from the CSP. The guest recovers the signer and publishes its address as the batch’s census root, which the registry compares with the process’s CSP address. The origin’s on-chain name, <0>CSP_EDDSA_BABYJUBJUB_V1</0>, is historical. A signed credential cannot be revoked inside the circuit."
msgstr "Each voter presents an ECDSA (secp256k1) signature from the CSP. The guest recovers the signer and publishes its address as the batch’s census root, which the registry compares with the process’s CSP address. The origin’s on-chain name, <0>CSP_EDDSA_BABYJUBJUB_V1</0>, is historical. A signed credential cannot be revoked inside the circuit."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "Each voter’s ballot is kept in one place in the election’s state, its <0>slot</0>. A new vote from the same voter goes to the same slot and replaces the old ballot. With a list of voters the slot is computed from the voter’s address; with a credential service, from the number the service signs."
msgstr "Each voter’s ballot is kept in one place in the election’s state, its <0>slot</0>. A new vote from the same voter goes to the same slot and replaces the old ballot. With a list of voters the slot is computed from the voter’s address; with a credential service, from the number the service signs."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "The slot is on purpose not the voter’s position in the list. The proof of membership does not fix that position, and it would change as the list grows. A slot computed from the address stays put however the list grows."
msgstr "The slot is on purpose not the voter’s position in the list. The proof of membership does not fix that position, and it would change as the list grows. A slot computed from the address stays put however the list grows."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "Two voters on one slot would replace each other’s ballots, so slots must be unique. A contract that keeps a list refuses a registration whose slot is taken, sequencers refuse any list with two voters on one slot, and the batch proof refuses two votes for one slot in a batch. Making a clash on purpose would take about <0/> key generations in a list of <1/> members."
msgstr "Two voters on one slot would replace each other’s ballots, so slots must be unique. A contract that keeps a list refuses a registration whose slot is taken, sequencers refuse any list with two voters on one slot, and the batch proof refuses two votes for one slot in a batch. Making a clash on purpose would take about <0/> key generations in a list of <1/> members."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "With a Merkle census the slot comes from the voter’s address:"
msgstr "With a Merkle census the slot comes from the voter’s address:"

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
msgid "A CSP census uses <0/>, with the index the CSP signs. A lean-IMT proof does not bind the leaf index, so a proof of one leaf also verifies at other indexes, and a slot taken from the position would let a growing tree move voters between slots."
msgstr "A CSP census uses <0/>, with the index the CSP signs. A lean-IMT proof does not bind the leaf index, so a proof of one leaf also verifies at other indexes, and a slot taken from the position would let a growing tree move voters between slots."

#: src/pages/learn/content/Census.tsx
