#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "While voting is open you may vote again, and the new ballot replaces the old one. Every batch also refreshes ballots nobody changed, so a changed vote looks like routine upkeep. The published data shows when a voter’s ballot place is first used, but not whether that voter changed their vote later."
msgstr "While voting is open you may vote again, and the new ballot replaces the old one. Every batch also refreshes ballots nobody changed, so a changed vote looks like routine upkeep. The published data shows when a voter’s ballot place is first used, but not whether that voter changed their vote later."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "Voting again"
msgstr "Voting again"

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "A voter may vote again while the election is open. The new ballot goes to the same <0>slot</0> and replaces the old one: the batch proof takes the old ballot out of the encrypted total and adds the new one. The registry counts how many different voters have voted and how many votes were changed (<1>changed votes</1>)."
msgstr "A voter may vote again while the election is open. The new ballot goes to the same <0>slot</0> and replaces the old one: the batch proof takes the old ballot out of the encrypted total and adds the new one. The registry counts how many different voters have voted and how many votes were changed (<1>changed votes</1>)."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "Voting again only helps a voter if nobody can tell whose ballot was replaced. Two mechanisms make that so: re-encryption, and silent refreshes of ballots the batch did not change."
msgstr "Voting again only helps a voter if nobody can tell whose ballot was replaced. Two mechanisms make that so: re-encryption, and silent refreshes of ballots the batch did not change."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "The registry’s counters are <0>votersCount</0>, the distinct slots written, and <1>overwrittenVotesCount</1>."
msgstr "The registry’s counters are <0>votersCount</0>, the distinct slots written, and <1>overwrittenVotesCount</1>."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "Before a ballot is stored, the sequencer changes how its encryption looks without changing what it says (<0>re-encryption</0>). Without the batch’s secret, nobody can match the ballot a voter sent to the one stored, so a voter cannot prove which ballot their slot holds."
msgstr "Before a ballot is stored, the sequencer changes how its encryption looks without changing what it says (<0>re-encryption</0>). Without the batch’s secret, nobody can match the ballot a voter sent to the one stored, so a voter cannot prove which ballot their slot holds."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "It does not hide whose slot it is: the sequencer that sealed the batch knows the secret, and with a list of voters the slot follows from the voter’s address."
msgstr "It does not hide whose slot it is: the sequencer that sealed the batch knows the secret, and with a list of voters the slot follows from the voter’s address."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "The secret exists in the sequencer’s memory for one batch only and is never written or logged. The prover deletes its copy of the input after proving only when it runs without <0>DAVINCI_KEEP_INPUTS=1</0>. That is an operator setting you have to trust; nothing on the chain shows it."
msgstr "The secret exists in the sequencer’s memory for one batch only and is never written or logged. The prover deletes its copy of the input after proving only when it runs without <0>DAVINCI_KEEP_INPUTS=1</0>. That is an operator setting you have to trust; nothing on the chain shows it."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "Re-encryption adds an encryption of zero with a fresh random scalar to every ciphertext; the plaintext does not change. The scalars come from one secret seed per batch, drawn from the operating system’s randomness, through a SHA-256 chain that also takes the state root before the batch. Every element is used once, so no scalar repeats within a transition or across them. The guest recomputes the chain from the seed and verifies every re-encryption."
msgstr "Re-encryption adds an encryption of zero with a fresh random scalar to every ciphertext; the plaintext does not change. The scalars come from one secret seed per batch, drawn from the operating system’s randomness, through a SHA-256 chain that also takes the state root before the batch. Every element is used once, so no scalar repeats within a transition or across them. The guest recomputes the chain from the seed and verifies every re-encryption."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "Silent refreshes"
msgstr "Silent refreshes"

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "Every batch also refreshes some stored ballots it did not change: the same re-encryption, applied in place, so what they say stays the same. The sequencer picks them at random, never from public data, because a choice anyone could compute would let them take the refreshes out and spot the changed votes."
msgstr "Every batch also refreshes some stored ballots it did not change: the same re-encryption, applied in place, so what they say stays the same. The sequencer picks them at random, never from public data, because a choice anyone could compute would let them take the refreshes out and spot the changed votes."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "A batch must refresh at least 16 ballots, at least twice as many as its changed votes and at least as many as its votes (the requirement stops at 2048), or every stored ballot it did not change when there are fewer. The refreshed ballots are written back like any other, and their refreshes are added to the encrypted total as well, so the total moves with every ballot the batch touched."
msgstr "A batch must refresh at least 16 ballots, at least twice as many as its changed votes and at least as many as its votes (the requirement stops at 2048), or every stored ballot it did not change when there are fewer. The refreshed ballots are written back like any other, and their refreshes are added to the encrypted total as well, so the total moves with every ballot the batch touched."

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "Refreshes re-randomize occupied slots the batch did not write, with no change of plaintext, and the guest adds their encryptions of zero to the accumulator. The guest requires at least this many refreshes per batch:"
msgstr "Refreshes re-randomize occupied slots the batch did not write, with no change of plaintext, and the guest adds their encryptions of zero to the accumulator. The guest requires at least this many refreshes per batch:"

#: src/pages/learn/content/SilentRevoting.tsx
msgid "with"
msgstr "with"

