#: src/pages/sequencers/explain.tsx
msgid "What a sequencer does"
msgstr "What a sequencer does"

#: src/pages/sequencers/explain.tsx
msgid "It collects the encrypted ballots and checks each one the way the proof will: the ballot’s own proof, the signature, the voter’s place on the list of voters and the inputs hash."
msgstr "It collects the encrypted ballots and checks each one the way the proof will: the ballot’s own proof, the signature, the voter’s place on the list of voters and the inputs hash."

#: src/pages/sequencers/explain.tsx
msgid "It groups them into batches, re-encrypts every ballot, and re-encrypts a sample of other ballots already stored (silent refreshes), so nobody can tell who changed their vote."
msgstr "It groups them into batches, re-encrypts every ballot, and re-encrypts a sample of other ballots already stored (silent refreshes), so nobody can tell who changed their vote."

#: src/pages/sequencers/explain.tsx
msgid "It has the batch proven as one proof (a ZisK PLONK)."
msgstr "It has the batch proven as one proof (a ZisK PLONK)."

#: src/pages/sequencers/explain.tsx
msgid "It records the batch on the registry in one transaction, with the data anyone needs to rebuild the state attached as data blobs."
msgstr "It records the batch on the registry in one transaction, with the data anyone needs to rebuild the state attached as data blobs."

#: src/pages/sequencers/explain.tsx
msgid "How it fits together"
msgstr "How it fits together"

#: src/pages/sequencers/explain.tsx
msgid "Why several can serve one election"
msgstr "Why several can serve one election"

#: src/pages/sequencers/explain.tsx
msgid "The registry takes a batch from anyone, as long as its proof is valid and it starts from the current state. When two nodes race, the first transaction lands and the other fails on that check. The loser rebuilds the winner’s batch from its published data, queues its own votes again and builds on the new state. A race costs gas; no vote is lost and the state stays consistent."
msgstr "The registry takes a batch from anyone, as long as its proof is valid and it starts from the current state. When two nodes race, the first transaction lands and the other fails on that check. The loser rebuilds the winner’s batch from its published data, queues its own votes again and builds on the new state. A race costs gas; no vote is lost and the state stays consistent."

#: src/pages/sequencers/explain.tsx
msgid "A node without a key, an <0>observer</0>, follows every election the same way and answers questions about it (receipts for votes included), but never records a batch. With a sequencer key, only the node that handed out the key can publish that election’s results."
msgstr "A node without a key, an <0>observer</0>, follows every election the same way and answers questions about it (receipts for votes included), but never records a batch. With a sequencer key, only the node that handed out the key can publish that election’s results."

#: src/pages/sequencers/index.tsx
msgid "Who records the votes"
msgstr "Who records the votes"

#: src/pages/sequencers/index.tsx
msgid "A <0>sequencer</0> collects the encrypted ballots, proves them in batches and records each batch on the registry, which checks the proof before accepting it. Anyone can run one: the registry takes a batch from any account, as long as its proof is valid."
msgstr "A <0>sequencer</0> collects the encrypted ballots, proves them in batches and records each batch on the registry, which checks the proof before accepting it. Anyone can run one: the registry takes a batch from any account, as long as its proof is valid."

